### Using the information to solve

In order to find $c'(t_0)$ we need to find $c(t_0)$. Using the Pythagorean theorem and the fact that $a(t_0)=24$ and $b=10$, we can find that $c(t_0)=26$.

Now let's plug $\blueD{a(t_0)}=\blueD{24}$, $\maroonD{a'(t_0)}=\maroonD{-13}$, and $\goldD{c(t_0)}=\goldD{26}$ into the expression for $c'(t_0)$:

$\begin{align}
c'(t_0)&=\dfrac{\blueD{a(t_0)}\maroonD{a'(t_0)}}{\goldD{c(t_0)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(\blueD{24})(\maroonD{-13})}{(\goldD{26})}
\\\\
&=-12
\end{align}$
